[{:seon.task/name "a-thread-wakes-its-joiners-before-it-leaves-the-live-threads"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-11-09 by the main-carrier stuck fix (Jolt 869d77f6), left open on purpose rather than patched: a thread's exit (reference-code/jolt/host/chez/lazy-bridge.ss, the exit path near jolt-thread-exit-hook and jolt-thread-end-wake) removes it from live-threads and only after that wakes the threads and fibers joining it, so between the two a deadlock check sees no live thread that may run while a joiner is about to be woken, and can tell that joiner a false deadlock. The patch the fix declined was counting a thread mid-exit as able to run (a special case in the detector). The cause is the order: an ending thread wakes its joiners first and leaves live-threads after, both under the lock the detector reads with, so no check can see the gap. The comment there explains why live-threads is updated before the exit hook (a late parent re-adding an entry); keep that guarantee. Proof, red first: a fiber on the main carrier that joins a thread ending in that window is answered by the join, never told a deadlock (force the window as test/chez/main-carrier-stuck-test.clj forces its)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{jolt.host-test/fiber-main-carrier-joins-thread-ending-exit-window}}
 {:seon.task/name "jolt-is-at-upstreams-head"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: Deep fixes. The owner's ruling (docs/decisions.md, 2026-11-10; docs/forks.md#following-upstream). On main: Jolt pinned at `122445b2`, the tip of ``jolt/on-upstream-2026-11-10`` (upstream main `8d3ba3c9` with our commits merged on top), proved whole at its landing. What remains: Jolt's own gates, red on the pin's line until ``jolt/the-red-gates-are-read`` (2026-11-12, cut from `de563eba`), where every gate that branch touched is green and the red list (test/forks/jolt-gates-red.txt) names one: buildsmoke (task jolt.backend/build-smoke-is-green-on-the-pin). What that branch found: parkcheck had one real fault (ac-close! ran the program's uncaught handler under a channel's mutex) and otherwise read a spawned thunk as its spawner's call; fiberspoll's and smoke's socket case were this machine's kernel (macOS 27: SO_LINGER over 32767 is EDOM, a dial to a full queue is reset), now skipped with the reason printed; smoke's malformed-let row expected the old wording where the analyzer gives the spec's, as JVM Clojure 1.12 does; largebytesgc was ours (a byte array coerced from a byte array was copied mobile; now through the immobile copy); recordinline, dcerefs, certify, gambitgencheck and gosm were stale against our own commits; deadhost two uncalled definitions. portcheck and attributioncheck are upstream's policies, not run (each says why in test/forks/jolt-gates.txt). The whole table is not yet rerun on that line. Also: the fork repo's ``jolt/main`` is at `1fad8b83`, under the pin; move it to the pin's line. Not ours to weaken: by the thread law (docs/machine.md) work the old Jolt ran on fibers and the new one runs on OS threads is fixed or configured in the fork, never added to the declared set. Done means: each gate green on the pin, or stated in docs/forks.md#jolt as not ours to hold with why, so a pin's report can say every gate green or name what is not. Proof to write: jolt.host-test/each-red-gate-is-green-or-stated-with-its-reason."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{jolt.host-test/each-red-gate-is-green-or-stated-with-its-reason}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-host-method-call-costs-a-dispatch"
  :seon.task/plan "Measured 2026-11-11 while cutting hasch: a method call on a host object costs about 190 ticks on Jolt (`.reset` on a `MessageDigest` alone 187; a SHA-512 of 2,368 bytes through libcrypto is 573), so a loop that calls one host method an element is dominated by dispatch (hasch's digest of 64 uuids: 19.3k of its ticks). A plain protocol method call on a deftype costs 10-13 ticks and allocates nothing (one type at the site), a call of five or more arguments 9 ticks and 80 B plus 16 B an argument (the same on upstream's head). So the 80-208 B a method call seen inside the sorted set, which the waived proof for `seon.db/eid` blames on a deftype method call's site cache (task jolt.backend/a-deftype-method-call-takes-a-site-cache), is more likely a host method call or a five-argument call: not yet measured there. Correct: name which call shape the sorted set's inner loop pays (a profile of 1,000 seeks on the Jolt at upstream's head), correct that waiver's stated cause with it, then cut the shape at its cause (a host site's lookup, or the argument list a wide call builds). Should cost: a host method call within a few times a protocol call."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-fibers-own-state-lives-in-its-slice"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: Deep fixes. The second of four Jolt pins that make a fiber's state safe by construction (docs/forks.md#a-fibers-state-is-safe-by-construction; design approved 2026-11-11). What exists: the scheduler saves and restores three thread parameters at every pause (the binding stack, the namespace, the transaction: ``host/chez/fibers.ss``, the dslice); everything else kept per OS thread is shared by the fibers of that thread. Shown red 2026-11-11 on the Jolt branch ``jolt/a-fibers-state-is-its-own`` (``test/chez/fiber-safety.clj``): an eval's byte budget counts the carrier's allocation, so an eval is told it spent its budget for another fiber's allocation (``java/concurrency.ss`` near 2217; Seon reads it in seon.intr); a ``ThreadLocal`` set by one fiber is read by another (``java/host-static-classes.ss`` near 3219); the error-position state is per carrier. Correct: ONE macro, ``define-fiber-state``, is the only way to make state a fiber owns; it adds a slice field the switch saves and restores; ThreadLocal, the byte count and the error position become slice fields; a foreign call's errno is returned with its result inside a region no pause can enter. Gate: a thread parameter or virtual register written by a setter outside the scheduler fails. Cost to measure: ns a slice field adds to a pause. Proof when built: the byte-budget and threadlocal cases of that file green, as a gate of the fork."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-scoped-construct-has-no-entry-hook"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: Deep fixes. The third of the four pins (docs/forks.md#a-fibers-state-is-safe-by-construction). What exists: a pause unwinds and a resume rewinds every ``dynamic-wind`` but a ``finally``, so a before-thunk that sets state runs again on resume. Measured 2026-11-11: a check at every resume that the slice and the winder chain are the objects saved at the pause raised nowhere in Jolt's unit suite and 15 fiber gates; what the compiler and the core macros emit is right; the offenders are host sites: ``jolt-with-out-str`` (fixed, Jolt `ec4c1e44`), the AOT open-compile mark (``host/chez/loader.ss`` near 2033 and 2092: a pause closes the batch and a queued cache write is lost, shown red), and the build-only ``jolt-ns-load-vars-push!``. Correct: four constructors that take no entry hook from their caller (fiber-scoped over the slice, exit-only cleanup as a ``finally``, OS-thread-scoped by parameterize, held mutex), and raw ``dynamic-wind`` nowhere else in the host. Gate: ``dynamic-wind`` and ``jolt-wind*`` appear only inside those four definitions. The resume check stays a finder (5 ns on a 310 ns pause, 1.7%: over the 1% bar for always-on), off in shipped builds. Proof when built: the aot-batch-park case green and the gate."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-shared-table-is-frozen-swapped-or-locked-inside"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: Deep fixes. The fourth of the four pins (docs/forks.md#a-fibers-state-is-safe-by-construction). What exists: the class tables (``class-ctors-tbl``, ``class-statics-tbl``, ``host-class-ctors-tbl``, ``host-class-statics-tbl``, ``host-methods-tbl``, ``host-arity-wrappers``, ``jhost-tag->fqn``: ``java/host-static.ss``) are written by deftype, defrecord and lazy class loads with no lock, and the epoch is bumped before the write. Measured 2026-11-11 with a finder that raises on a write outside the registry's mutex: 99 raises at 6 sites in the unit suite; with the finder off, a racing test lost a record type's constructor in 1 of 6 runs and stalled in 2 of 6 (the stall's cause is not found: find it first). Also unlocked: the jar-entry weak tables, the FFI size table's reads, smaller registries and two once-only latches (the sweep's findings 9 to 12). Correct, per table, one of three forms and no other: filled before a second fiber or thread exists; an immutable value in one box replaced by compare-and-set (reads take no lock); or, for a weak table or a hot write path, a table type whose lock is inside its operations and whose raw table is not reachable. Gate: a process-wide mutable defined any other way fails. Proof when built: the deftype-race case green 100 of 100, and the gate."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-deep-call-with-a-collection-inside-sometimes-counts-96-bytes"
  :seon.task/plan "Measured 2026-11-11 on Chez 4d318683 and Jolt 7135d660: a window of the calling thread's allocated bytes around a 20,000-deep call that asks for a collection at its deepest frame reads 0 B, and in about one window of 200 reads 96 B (1 of 400, 1 of 150, 1 of 10 before the two fixes, when it read 704 B against 608 B). The same count of windows around the deep call alone (600) and around a collection alone (350) never read it, so it needs both: a stack the collection made one multi-shot continuation, and something else landing while it is cut on the way back. Inferred, not shown: the engine's tick handler capturing a continuation on that stack (64 B and a 32 B closure). Reproduction: seon.profile-test/a-new-stack-is-not-the-calls-bytes with its inside window taken 400 times and counted by value. Correct: the source found by reading what allocates 96 B on that path (``S_split_and_resize``, the tick handler in Chez's s/library.ss), then left out of the thread's count where it is made if it is the runtime's own; the test then takes one window, not the least of three."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.profile-test/a-new-stack-is-not-the-calls-bytes}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-lazy-nodes-claim-allocates-nothing-when-nobody-contends"
  :seon.task/plan "Half done, half held. DONE in Jolt 6a8df52a (on main with pin 75eec622): a forced lazy node or cell allocates nothing for its claim on the safe path (one claimer an owner, reused), so the regression Jolt c5e8be67 landed with is taken back: the same agent eval of identical source (the form is in docs/cost.md#how-we-measure), five runs each, interleaved: 88.7 MB on the pin against 105.4 MB on main before it, and 88.3 MB before any fiber fix; ticks 20.2M against 20.4M. What remains is the flag: ``jolt-mt?`` still chooses between a fast path and the safe one at five sites and is read by ten memory fences, which need only 'a second OS thread exists'. HELD on the owner's answer to one question (asked 2026-11-11): may the fork delete the single-threaded fast paths outright? No Seon process runs them any more (every root, agent, test child and shard has a fiber), so it costs Seon nothing; a one-thread Jolt script would run up to 43% more ticks on a hand-written lazy seq (20.0M against 14.0M a million nodes, measured), and the fork would differ from upstream on its own benchmarks. Yes: the five fast-path sites and the flag go, the fences read a flag set only where an OS thread is made, and no new fast path can be written against a flag that is not there. No: the two meanings are split and a gate holds the readers to one constructor. Nothing of either is built until he answers. Owner: Deep fixes."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-vars-first-watch-is-never-missed"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-11-12 beside the atom ordering fix (Jolt de563eba, docs/forks.md#jolt). ``def-var!`` reads the count of watched references before it stores the var's root, so a thread adding the first watch of the process on that var while another defines it can miss the change even with the stores ordered: the JVM's ``bindRoot`` stores the root, then notifies. Read in host/chez/atoms.ss (``iref-notify``, the side table's counter) and the var's definition path; not reproduced. Seon watches vars in ``seon.render/walk`` (a render's redefinition draws it again), from the one thread that owns the walk, so no path of ours is known to meet it. First: a two-thread case in Jolt's gate ``atomwatchorder`` for a var (add-watch then deref against a def), red if the fault is real; then the store before the read."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "two-gates-glued-in-the-makefile-run"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-11-12: both gate lists of reference-code/jolt's Makefile (JOLT-TARGETS-NEEDING-DEPS and CI-GATES) hold the one word ``buildemptycacheentryaotcachepathsmoke``: the gates ``buildemptycacheentry`` (ours, Jolt 75eec622) and ``aotcachepathsmoke`` lost the space between them when the first was added, so neither runs from the lists (``make ci``); ``bin/jolt-gates`` runs each by the name in test/forks/jolt-gates.txt, so Seon's own pin check is not affected (verify: both names are in that file). The fix is the space, in the next Jolt commit, with ``make -n ci`` naming both."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-running-fibers-dump-entry-names-where-it-was-spawned"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-11-12: a system on main ran one core for 16 minutes (the evidence: the sessions' notes folder, hang-pairclean-0320/). Jolt's fiber dump (kill -INFO) showed the two fibers that held the work, one with 57.6 billion ticks parked at that instant as 'a CPS step: the procedure ?' and one with 54.3 billion 'running, no saved continuation', and named neither: a running fiber has no continuation to print, and a go block's step prints no source. So the dump that exists to name a spinning loop could not, and the native sample named only Chez's own frames. What a dump entry needs: where the fiber was spawned (the namespace and line of its go block or spawn call, kept on the fiber record at spawn: one word a fiber, read only by the dump), printed for every fiber, running ones first. Read fibers.ss's dump (jolt-fiber-dump) and the spawn seams (fibers-async.ss, async.ss's go) first; the cost bound is one field write a spawn. Proof: a gate case in siginfodump: a fiber spinning in a named go block is named by file and line in the dump."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-fiber-dump-reads-each-fibers-state-once"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: Deep fixes. jolt-fiber-dump (reference-code/jolt/host/chez/fibers.ss) reads a fiber's state once for its carrier's line (running fiber N / running no fiber) and again for the fiber's own line, so a busy fiber can show 'running' on a carrier that says it runs no fiber. On 2026-11-12 that read as a lost wake and sent the problems-step bisect the wrong way; the hang was a Seon error loop (jolt/tests-apiloop). Read each fiber's state and carrier once into the numbered list and print both lines from it. Proof, red first: a siginfodump case with a fiber busy across the dump never shows it 'running' on a carrier line that says 'running no fiber'. Where the dump is described: `docs/forks.md#jolt` (diagnostics) and `docs/machine.md#reading-a-hang`."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{jolt.host-test/fiber-dump-shows-a-busy-fiber-on-its-own-carrier}}
 {:seon.task/name "fork-child-test-forks-from-a-one-thread-parent"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: Deep fixes. test/chez/fork-child-test.clj (Jolt) forks with jolt.host/fork-alone from a process whose timer, poller and carriers it started itself, so fork-alone refuses (-2) whenever one of those threads is active at that instant; the fibers gate went red once so (Jolt 0f78c7b3, load 100+). fork-alone is right: -2 is its typed refusal, and Seon forks only from seon.fork-server, which is one thread. No wait is added. The test forks each check from a fresh one-thread parent, so a -2 there is a real bug. Proof, red first: the test with a carrier kept busy beside each fork, red now, green after. How a Seon process forks: `docs/forks.md#jolt` (fork safety) and `docs/processes.md`."
